---------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. THE SAFETY PROPERTY — THE THREE DELETION GUARDS — PASS
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
This is where a bug destroys user data, so it got the most scrutiny, by three
independent methods: static reachability, test instrumentation, and a live
server.

(a) Static reachability. There is exactly ONE source-deleting call site in the
package. `grep -n 'removeSrc' internal/handlers/explorer/handlers.go`:
    538:	removeSrc  func(path string) error          (field decl)
    550:	removeSrc:  os.Remove,                       (production binding)
    696:	if err := deps.removeSrc(srcPath); err != nil {   (the only call)
`grep -n 'os.Remove\|os.RemoveAll'` over the package finds no other removal of
an import-directory path. So reaching line 696 is the whole safety question.

Every branch between the entry of importOneFile (handlers.go:616) and line 696
is an early `return`, so line 696 is reachable only with all preconditions
satisfied. Walking the guards in order:

  Step 1, handlers.go:621-624 — name validation with NO filesystem call:
      if name == "" || name == "." || name == ".." ||
          name != filepath.Base(name) || strings.ContainsAny(name, `/\`)
    returns "rejected: invalid filename". Both separator flavours are covered,
    and "." / ".." are excluded by hand (they are their own filepath.Base, so
    the Base comparison alone would not catch them). Correct.
  handlers.go:628 — non-.csv suffix rejected, still before any filesystem
    access.
  handlers.go:632 — empty ImportDirectory rejected.
  Step 2, handlers.go:640-653 — os.Lstat (NOT os.Stat), so a symlink is never
    followed; explicit rejects for symlink (645), non-regular file (648), and
    !isDirectChild (651)  <-- GUARD 3.
  Step 3, handlers.go:661-665 — destination exists => "skipped" return.
    Notably a Stat error that is NOT os.ErrNotExist is rejected rather than
    treated as absent (663-664), so a permission/IO error cannot be misread as
    "absent" and lead to an overwrite-then-delete. This is a correctness point
    the spec did not demand and the implementation got right.
  Step 4, handlers.go:668-674 — read source; write via store.WriteFile;
    a write error returns "rejected"  <-- GUARD 1.
  Step 5, handlers.go:679-685 — readback error returns "rejected", and
    `if len(back) != len(data)` returns "rejected: saved file failed
    verification"  <-- GUARD 2.
  handlers.go:688-691 — if !deleteSource, return before step 6.
  Step 6, handlers.go:696 — os.Remove reached only here.

Conclusion: no path reaches os.Remove with any guard unsatisfied. A skipped
(collision) file returns at 662 and a rejected (traversal / non-CSV / symlink /
subdirectory) file returns at 623/629/646/652 — all strictly before 696.

isDirectChild (handlers.go:713-723) resolves symlinks on BOTH sides via
filepath.EvalSymlinks and returns false when either resolution errors, then
compares filepath.Dir(resolvedPath) == resolvedDir. Because callers Lstat and
reject symlinks first, EvalSymlinks on the path can only resolve its parent
components — so a symlinked import folder still works while anything reached
by leaving the folder does not. Both directions are pinned by tests.

(b) Every one of the three guards is EXERCISED BY A TEST that instruments
removeSrc and asserts it was never called. My brief asked me to flag any guard
present in code but not exercised; there are none. Specifically:
  - Guard 1 (write succeeded): TestImportOneFile_WriteFailureNeverDeletes,
    handlers_test.go:2573. Injects os.ErrPermission into deps.write, asserts
    `removed` is false ("os.Remove was reached despite a failed write") and
    mustExist(src).
  - Guard 2 (readback matched): TestImportOneFile_ShortReadbackNeverDeletes,
    handlers_test.go:2598. deps.readBack returns importCSV[:10] — a genuinely
    short read, the truncated/encryption-failed-write scenario the design doc
    calls out at line 149. Asserts removed==false and the source survives, and
    deliberately asserts the destination DID land, making clear the property
    under test is source survival, not destination absence. Companion
    TestImportOneFile_ReadbackErrorNeverDeletes at 2625 covers the error arm.
  - Guard 3 (direct child): TestImportOneFile_SubdirectoryEntryNeverDeletes,
    handlers_test.go:2647, drives BOTH spellings "sub/nested.csv" (rejected by
    name) and "nested.csv" (rejected as not a direct child), asserting one
    shared removed==false. Plus TestIsDirectChild at 2847, a table covering
    direct child, grandchild, traversal sibling, outside dir, nonexistent
    path, nonexistent dir, and the symlinked-import-dir positive case.
This directly satisfies the design doc's Testing bullet "a `store` stub whose
readback returns short bytes must leave the source in place" (line 308-309),
which the report.md adjudication cites as the deciding factor for wt-local. I
confirmed the claim rather than taking it on trust.

(c) Live server, mixed batch — the strongest single piece of evidence. I built
the binary and ran it against fixture directories entirely outside the repo
(details in item 8). A single batch mixing one importable file, one colliding
file, and one non-CSV, all with delete_source=true:

  POST name=delta.csv&name=collide.csv&name=notes.txt&delete_source=true
  -> HTTP 200
  delta src: DELETED | collide src: PRESENT | notes.txt: PRESENT | keepme: PRESENT

Exactly the imported file's source was removed; the skipped file, the rejected
file, and an uninvolved bystander (keepme.csv) were all untouched. Across the
entire live run, of ten POSTs the ONLY two sources deleted were beta.csv and
delta.csv — precisely the two files that fully imported with the literal
delete_source=true. The final `ls` of the import dir confirms alpha.csv (no
delete flag), gamma.csv (delete_source=TRUE, not literal), collide.csv
(skipped), notes.txt (rejected), link.csv (symlink, rejected), subdir/ and
keepme.csv all survived.

