7. ACCESSIBILITY OF THE WIRED PANEL — PASS (against ACCESSIBILITY.md)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Point 8 (meaning never conveyed by color alone). PASS, and verified from the
  real response bytes rather than by reading the template. Every outcome
  carries its status as a WORD: the live bodies contain "— imported",
  "— skipped", "— rejected", plus a parenthesised text reason
  ("source file deleted", "already exists in the data folder", "only CSV files
  can be imported", "invalid filename", "symlinks are not imported"). The
  Tailwind emerald/amber/rose classes at filemanager.html:1462 are a redundant
  second cue, never the only one. Whether the source was deleted is likewise
  stated in text, not left to be inferred from the checkbox state.
- Point 10 (state-changing actions announce their result via an
  aria-live="polite" region). PASS. filemanager.html:102 is
  `<div id="import-result" role="status" aria-live="polite"></div>` — both
  role="status" and an explicit aria-live. It is present on first paint, so
  the live region is already being watched when the swap lands (an empty
  region added at swap time is frequently not announced). It sits OUTSIDE the
  <form>, and hx-target="#import-result" (line 81) targets only it, so the
  import never replaces the controls that triggered it and keyboard focus
  survives the swap — which is the other half of point 10.
- Point 4 (every input programmatically associated with a label). PASS. The
  delete-source checkbox has BOTH an explicit association and a wrapping
  label: `<label for="import-delete-source">` at line 86 around
  `<input type="checkbox" id="import-delete-source" name="delete_source">` at
  line 88. Each scan entry likewise pairs `id="import-entry-{{$i}}"` (1434)
  with `<label for="import-entry-{{$i}}">` (1437).
- Point 3 (links navigate, buttons act; anything clickable is a real control).
  PASS. Import is `<button type="submit">` (line 92-95) and Rescan is
  `<button type="button">` (line 64). No div-with-handler anywhere in the
  panel. The previous inert `disabled aria-disabled="true"` Import button and
  its "Import is not yet available" title were correctly removed when it was
  wired.
- Point 9 (visible focus indicator; never outline:none without replacement).
  PASS. Both buttons and both checkbox styles use
  `focus:outline-none focus:ring-2 focus:ring-indigo-500` — the native outline
  is suppressed but replaced by a 2px indigo ring, which is a replacement, not
  a removal.
- Point 1 (heading hierarchy). PASS. The panel is a <section
  aria-labelledby="import-folder-heading"> whose <h2 id="import-folder-heading">
  sits under the page's single <h1>. No level skipped.
No accessibility regression is introduced by P15. The 20 pre-existing axe
violations recorded in the design doc under "Known, not caused by P13"
(unlabelled toggle checkboxes, unnamed SVG delete buttons, low-contrast size
text) live elsewhere on this page and are untouched by this diff; per that
note they belong to a follow-up task.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. REGRESSION GUARD PER RULING 2026-08-16a — PASS
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Ruling 2026-08-16a imposes two obligations. Both are met, and I deliberately
did not repeat the earlier checker's mistake of reasoning from a template
include.

Obligation 2 ("must ship a Go test that renders the swap partial and asserts
on it"): TestImportResult_Render_Outcomes, handlers_test.go:2795, is that
test. It renders the "import-result" partial — the exact template name
handleImport passes to RenderPartial at handlers.go:601 — and asserts nine
substrings across all three outcome kinds. Two properties make it a genuine
guard rather than a restatement:
  - Its renderer is built from the REAL template tree, not a stub:
    setupTestEnvWithRenderer (handlers_test.go:1301) does
    `templateDir := filepath.Join(testutil.ProjectRoot(), "web", "templates")`
    then `templates.New(templateDir, false)` (lines 1318-1319).
  - RenderToString and RenderPartial share one lookup mechanism. Both bottom
    out in `r.templates.ExecuteTemplate(..., name, data)` — RenderPartial at
    internal/templates/render.go:397, RenderToString at render.go:409 — on the
    same parsed template set. So a lookup that succeeds in the test succeeds
    in the handler. This is materially different from the P13 attempt-1
    situation, where the test rendered the full PAGE and therefore said
    nothing about what the handler returned.

Obligation 1 ("where feasible assert against the endpoint's actual response
body"): I did this directly, because it is the one thing neither the oracle
nor the Go suite covers. Note the residual risk that motivated it: the
handler discards the render error (`_ = renderer.RenderPartial(...)` at
handlers.go:601), so a failed template lookup would surface as HTTP 200 with
an empty body — invisible to accept.sh, which asserts only status codes and
filesystem effects.

Method (all artifacts outside the repo, in my scratchpad): built
`go build -o $SCRATCH/live/budget2 ./cmd/server`, created fixture data/import/
outside directories under $SCRATCH/live, started the server on port 18977 with
BUDGET_DATA_DIR/BUDGET2_IMPORT_DIR pointed at them, and issued ten POSTs with
curl capturing full response bodies.

The endpoint returns real, correctly rendered markup. Probe B verbatim:
    --- HTTP 200   body-bytes: 493
    <p class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Import finished: 1 file(s) processed.</p>
    <ul class="mt-1 space-y-1" role="list">
        <li class="text-sm text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
            <span class="font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">beta.csv</span>
            <span class="font-medium text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-300">— imported</span>
            <span class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">(source file deleted)</span>
        </li>
    </ul>
Bodies were 419-1178 bytes across the nine 200s, never empty, never the
"Internal Server Error" text that a failed RenderPartial would produce, and
the server log shows no "Error rendering partial" line. Startup logged
"Templates loaded successfully: 49 files". The fragment is a bare partial with
no <html>/base layout, confirming it is swap-ready for
hx-swap="innerHTML" into #import-result. The feature is genuinely wired: this
is behavior established from the handler and from the endpoint's real
response, not from a template include.

Server killed via the script's EXIT trap; `ss -ltnp` afterwards shows nothing
listening on 18942 or 18977. Post-run `git status --porcelain` is unchanged
from the pre-run state (see item 1) — I wrote nothing into the repository
except this verdict file.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NON-BLOCKING OBSERVATION (does not affect the verdict)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
All handleImport tests drive the JSON fallback branch: setupImportScanEnv
leaves `renderer` nil, so handlers.go:600-605 takes the `else` arm and the
tests decode JSON (decodeImportResult, handlers_test.go:2285). That is a
reasonable design — it makes per-file outcome assertions precise — and the
partial is separately covered by TestImportResult_Render_Outcomes. But it
means no Go test executes handlers.go:601 itself. Because the render error
there is discarded with `_ =`, a future edit that changed only the template
NAME in the handler (leaving the {{define}} and its test intact) would ship a
200-with-empty-body and the suite would stay green. I verified today's
behavior is correct by live request, so this is a durability gap in the test
net, not a defect in P15. Worth a cheap follow-up: one handler-level test that
calls setupTestEnvWithRenderer and asserts the recorder body contains
"Import finished", which would pin handlers.go:601 end to end.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONCLUSION
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
All eight criteria pass. The safety property in item 4 — the one where a bug
destroys the user's data — holds under static reachability analysis (one
delete call site, every guard an early return), under test instrumentation
(all three guards individually asserted never to reach os.Remove), and under a
live mixed-batch request where exactly the imported file's source was removed
and the skipped, rejected, and bystander files all survived.

VERDICT: PASS
